{
  "id": "every-saturday",
  "title": "Every Saturday",
  "level": "A2",
  "summary": "Every Saturday my family does the same happy things. What does your family do?",
  "teachingMethod": "Theme-based",
  "teachingMethodReason": "Theme-based rather than story-based: one theme (a family's weekly routine) organizes the whole book, and a single language pattern - an adverb of frequency plus the simple present - recycles on every page. Nothing actually happens: there is no problem, no complication and no resolution, so it fails the story-based test that a-day-at-the-park passes. It is not CLIL either, because no subject content is taught and no cause-and-effect reasoning is required. Chosen to carry adverbs of frequency, which no other book in the library uses, and to give A2 a present-tense habitual text alongside its past-tense narratives.",
  "grammarFeatures": [
    "Adverbs of frequency (always, usually, often, sometimes, never)",
    "Simple present for habits and routines",
    "Prepositions of place (at the table, on the bench, under the tree)",
    "Causal clause with \"because\"",
    "Sequencing across a day (First, Then, In the evening)"
  ],
  "introFunFacts": [
    "🔁 This whole book is about things that happen again and again, every single week.",
    "📊 Watch for always, usually, often, sometimes and never - they tell you HOW OFTEN something happens!",
    "🤔 'Because' is here too - it always tells you WHY."
  ],
